    "englishTranslation": "Establishing Epistemic Regularity (Part 1 — Three Models of Consciousness & Perception): The Dvaitin objects that if the empirical world is superimposed (ādhyāsika), there can be no fixed correspondence between sense-organ contact, object, and cognition (pratikarma-vyavasthā), as occurs in ordinary illusory shell-silver. Madhusūdana replies across all three classical Advaita models of the Jīva: (1) If Jīva-consciousness is all-pervading and uncovered, it becomes specifically tinted/associated (uparāga) with a particular object solely via a mental modification (antaḥkaraṇa-vṛtti) assuming that object's form—just as universal cowness is manifested only in an individual with a dewlap, or lamp-light illuminates color rather than odor; (2) if Jīva-consciousness is veiled by Avidyā, the outgoing vṛtti dispels the veil concealing the consciousness delimited by that object; (3) if Jīva-consciousness is limited by the internal organ, the vṛtti bridges and manifests the non-difference between knower-consciousness (pramātṛ) and object-consciousness (viṣaya-caitanya).\n\nNor does epistemic regularity collapse into subjective idealism (dṛṣṭisṛṣṭi / pratītimātra-śarīratva), because empirical stability (vyāvahārika-sthāyitva) of scrutinized objects like a merchant's silver versus a tested shell is grounded in non-sublation prior to Brahman-knowledge. Moreover, direct perceptual immediacy (aparokṣatva) arises not from an unassociated vṛtti-genus, which would confuse recognition ('this is that'), nor from inert vṛtti itself—which being insentient cannot illuminate objects without self-luminosity—but strictly from the manifestation of object-consciousness when the vṛtti travels out through the senses to the object.",
